\description{
\code{sigdots()} finds the spatial domains of a spatial transcriptomics sample that are
supported by the data. It starts from an over-segmentation of the sample into \code{K0}
\emph{candidate domains} (more than the number of domains you expect), arranges the candidate
domains in a tree by spatial adjacency and expression similarity, and tests the splits of
the tree from the top down, each against a model in which the part being split is a single
domain. A split is kept only when it is significant; the parts of the tree that are not
split further are the domains. The probability that the result contains any false split is
at most \code{alpha}. The section "How SigDoTS works" below describes each step.
}
\section{How SigDoTS works}{

\enumerate{
\item \strong{Candidate domains.} The spots are divided into \code{K0} candidate domains with
\code{cluster_method}, or taken from \code{candidate_labels}. SigDoTS never splits a candidate
domain; it decides which candidate domains belong together. It therefore assumes that each
candidate domain lies inside one true domain.
\item \strong{The tree.} Each spot is linked to its \code{k_nn} nearest spots (see \code{\link[=sigdots_control]{sigdots_control()}});
two candidate domains are neighbours when some of their spots are linked. Of all pairs of
neighbouring groups, the pair whose merge increases the within-group sum of squares the
least (Ward's criterion, computed on the means of the groups in the first \code{n_pcs}
principal components) is merged, and this is repeated until one group is left. The result
is a binary tree whose top is the whole sample and whose bottom ends are the candidate
domains; every merge in it joins two groups that touch in space.
\item \strong{The statistic.} The splits are tested from the top. The statistic of a split is the
within-group sum of squares of its two sides divided by the total sum of squares of the
part, summed over all genes: a number between 0 and 1, smaller for a clearer split.
\item \strong{The reference.} To judge whether the observed statistic is small, SigDoTS simulates
datasets for the spots of the part from a model in which the part is a single domain (step
5). Each simulated dataset is divided into as many candidate domains as the part has, with
the same \code{cluster_method}; its tree is built in the same way, and the statistic of its top
split is computed. Because the simulated data go through the same clustering and the same
tree building as the real data, the comparison accounts for the separation that clustering
finds even in data with only one domain.
\item \strong{The single-domain model.} In the model, every spot of the part has the same mean
expression, and the noise is correlated both between genes and between nearby spots.
\itemize{
\item The spatial correlation is described by two numbers estimated from the part's own data:
\code{eta}, the share of the noise variance that is spatially correlated (from \code{eta_min} to
1), and \code{ell}, the distance over which the correlation reaches (Matérn 3/2 correlation
function, in spot spacings).
\item They are estimated from pairs of spots in the same candidate domain, so that differences
between candidate domains are not taken for spatial correlation: after the mean of each
candidate domain is removed, the correlation between such pairs at each distance is
matched to the correlation the model predicts (\code{plot(fit, type = "correlogram")} shows
both).
\item The correlation between genes is then estimated from the data, with the candidate-domain
means and the fitted spatial correlation removed, as a few strong components (at most
\code{r_cap}) plus independent noise of equal variance.
\item Simulating from the model needs a factor of the spatial covariance matrix of the part's
spots, computed exactly or with the Vecchia approximation (\code{spatial_method}).
}
\item \strong{The decision.} The p-value (\code{pvalue_method}) is compared with the level of the test,
\code{alpha * (m - 1) / (n - 1)} for a part of m of the n spots. If it is at most the level,
the split is kept and both sides are tested in turn; otherwise the part is a domain. With
these levels, the probability that any kept split is false (the family-wise error rate) is
at most \code{alpha} (the scheme of Kimes et al., 2017).
\item \strong{Parts that are not tested.} A part with fewer than \code{min_node_size} spots, a part whose
split would leave fewer than \code{min_child_size} spots on a side, and a single candidate
domain are domains without a test. A part where the spatial parameters cannot be estimated
(too few pairs of spots in the same candidate domain) is left untested together with
everything below it, and its spots get no domain (\code{NA}); \code{sigdots()} warns. Where the
spots of a part fall into separate pieces on the nearest-neighbour graph, the pieces are
joined by their closest spots before the trees are built (see \code{\link[=summary.sigdots]{summary.sigdots()}}).
}
}

\section{Choosing K0}{

\code{K0} must be larger than the number of domains you expect, and fine enough that every
candidate domain lies inside one true domain, because a candidate domain that straddles two
true domains can never be split. About twice the expected number of domains is a reasonable
start. A very large \code{K0} makes the lower parts of the tree small: the spatial parameters need
about 80 or more spots in a part (on a regular grid of spots), and small parts have small
levels, so a Monte Carlo test there needs many simulated datasets to keep a split.
}

\section{Run time and memory}{

Measured on simulated data with 2,000 genes and three domains (\code{K0 = 5}, exact computation,
10 cores; peak memory of the whole run): 900 spots, 32 seconds and 3.5 GiB; 2,500 spots,
4.3 minutes and 6.1 GiB; 10,000 spots, 18 minutes and 19.8 GiB. With the default Vecchia
approximation, 2,500 spots took a median of 4.0 minutes (three domains) and 1.4 minutes (one
domain) on 10 cores. Beyond about 20,000 spots use the Vecchia approximation: the exact
computation at 50,176 spots took 12 hours and 206 GiB. The estimation of the spatial
parameters computes the distances between all pairs of spots of the part being tested, so
its memory grows with the square of the number of spots whatever \code{spatial_method} is: one
estimation with 200 genes on a part of 10,000 spots used 2.1 GiB, of 19,881 spots 7.4 GiB,
and of 40,000 spots 29 GiB.
}

\section{Warnings at the end of a run}{

\itemize{
\item \emph{The spatial parameters could not be estimated at ... part(s) of the tree}: a part has
too few spots for the estimation; it is marked \verb{[!] NOT TESTED} and its spots get no
domain (\code{NA}). A smaller \code{K0} makes the parts larger.
\item \emph{The estimated spatial range ell is at the upper end of its allowed range}: the true
range may be longer, which can make splits easier to keep; rerun with a wider \code{ell_range} in
\code{\link[=sigdots_control]{sigdots_control()}}.
\item \emph{The test would need more than B_cap simulated datasets}: raise \code{B_cap} in
\code{\link[=sigdots_control]{sigdots_control()}}, or accept that part as a domain.
}

Before computing anything, \code{sigdots()} checks its input and stops with a message that names
the argument at fault (for example, when \code{x} has as many columns as \code{coords} has rows, it
suggests \code{t(x)}). A simulated dataset that fails also stops the run, rather than being
dropped. Every message is explained in \code{vignette("faq")}.
}

\examples{
\donttest{
# about 20 seconds on one core
fit <- sigdots(sigdots_example$x, sigdots_example$coords, K0 = 5)
fit
summary(fit)
table(domain = domains(fit)$domain, true_region = sigdots_example$region)
plot(fit)
plot(fit, type = "tree")

# more simulated datasets per test (finer p-values), on 2 cores
fit2 <- sigdots(sigdots_example$x, sigdots_example$coords, K0 = 5,
                B_min = 50, B_max = 50, n_cores = 2)

# the normal approximation of the p-value
fit3 <- sigdots(sigdots_example$x, sigdots_example$coords, K0 = 5,
                pvalue_method = "normal_adaptive")
summary(fit3)
}
}
